00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
 
it's a moment of change  representatives of lebanon's protest  movement making their way to parliament  they walked past security forces who  once kept them out during massive  demonstrations in recent years  it's the first session of the newly  elected legislature and these first-time  mps are hoping to bring change to a  decades-old system  there is no democracy in lebanon with  the consociational and confessional  system but we are here to reinforce  democracy in lebanon and to reinforce  human rights we're gonna refuse any any  kind of compromises  compromises based on power sharing  between the political establishment have  long been how politics is done here  that's how parliamentary speaker nabi  beri was re-elected  he has been in the position for three  decades and for many a symbol of a  failed system riddled with corruption  but this time it was different bury  failed to get the endorsement of a  majority and one with the slimmest of  margins  a blow but symbolic  for those who are hoping for radical  change that hasn't happened change mps  couldn't win enough seats the status quo  didn't change  and it's lebanese leaders who have been  in power who are expected to have the  final say on what's next  the vote for a new speaker starts a  constitutional process that includes the  formation of a new government and in a  few months the election of a new  president but the current landscape in  parliament the lack of a clear-cut  majority means there could be protracted  negotiations  an extended political vacuum won't help  a state and an economy that has all but  collapsed  the parliament has failed over the years  to implement or undertake any economic  reforms for that matter and during the  crisis in fact the parliament obstructed  economic changes and economic reforms  politicians are also accused of  obstructing accountability for the  beirut port explosion that killed more  than 200 people two years ago  the state killed my brother they are  responsible and we want to know the  truth  those who are confronting the system no  democratic change cannot come overnight  but today's vote appears to be the  beginning senator al-jazeera beirut  let's bring in jawad adra who's a  lebanese political scientist and analyst  and the founder of information  international he joins us now live from  beirut what do you make of the  re-election of lebanon's  speaker a man who's been in this job for  30 years  well this is the seventh time and yes it  is you're correct  i think really the question is what  should we make of what is the future of  lebanon  and this is the issue i don't think the  uh  you know we all watched i think the  election it's the process is nice and  it's good that we  uh that you know we are in a middle  eastern country where we have election  every four years and we have a  parliament and so on but is this  democracy  this is a question is this the life  that we want  we are a bankrupt company  and we have the same ceos still running  the company  the country really is facing existential  threat and the question is  how do we  come out of this  you know  so  how can we stand up again that's the  question  what are people  going to think the people all those  protests we saw  uh in in beirut some of them quite  violent people thoroughly thoroughly fed  up with the political classes and their  standard of living  what are they going to make of this of  this new parliament where as you say  you've got the same  ceo running a bankrupt company  uh first i think we have to really look  at what happened uh in uh  two you know two and a half years ago  and uh  when the protest started  was it a revolution we called it saura  it was not a revolution and it was not  thorough so we have to really fix our  terminology  as if  do we have democracy no we don't have  democracy  as one one time dr house former prime  minister said he said in lebanon we have  a lot of freedom  but we have little democracy  we don't have accountability  the people are  marginalized the majority of the people  you have  we're talking about roughly four and a  half maybe million  uh residents in lebanon lebanese  residents  half of those have always been  marginalized they are poor  and now maybe they are benefiting a  little bit from the ngos that are  distributing some aid  so these guys  don't count in a way in the political  power  you have the middle class that has been  destroyed  and most of them have immigrated or are  you know secluded  we are left with a society that is not a  society really we are left with uh with  with a very  awkward  population  on a piece of land  we claim to be a society we claim to be  communities we're neither communities  nor the society and the point is do we  have the vision to rebuild this country  and to you know to have to have new  founding fathers yes you you paint a  very depressing picture  how does change come about though in  lebanon  we have to have a common vision are we  united in our vision how to come out of  this we're not  we claim you know we we are so proud be  at the political lords of lebanon all  the new guys who just entered the  parliament  when they they are proud to have to say  you we have foreign support  we have you know i have good connection  with this country for this country  instead of saying let's focus on us  we have to have a common vision in  lebanon  as lebanese  how to come out with this and then after  we start our own reform process we don't  ask for prescription from outside for  reform we don't need lectures from  the outside about you should reform we  know we should reform  but we have not done this and we are all  responsible as population and as  politicians  good to talk to you sir many thanks  indeed

